
  - The password is never stored, in any form. Only [38;5;153mlastUsername[39m, and only when opted in.
  - Anything read back out of storage is untrusted input — any script on the origin, the devtools console, or an XSS can
    write it. So values are validated on read against the schema, [38;5;153mlastUsername[39m is length-capped, and the restored value
    is only ever assigned to [38;5;153minput.value[39m, never to [38;5;153minnerHTML[39m. A restored preference cannot become script.
  - [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m is plaintext and readable by anyone with the device. A remembered username is a mild disclosure on a
    shared machine — which is what makes the opt-in checkbox load-bearing rather than decorative, and why unchecking it
    must actively erase the stored value rather than just stop writing.

  To be unambiguous: this is a UI convenience, [1mnot[0m "remember me" authentication. No tokens, no credentials, no session
  persistence.

  [1mLogin-form wiring.[0m [38;5;153mindex.html[39m gains a labeled checkbox ([38;5;153m#remember-username[39m) inside the form. [38;5;153mapp.js[39m gains two small
  behaviors: on load, if [38;5;153mrememberUsername[39m is true, check the box and pre-fill the username field from [38;5;153mlastUsername[39m; on
  successful validation at submit, persist both values, or clear them if the box is unchecked. Unchecking the box clears
  [38;5;153mlastUsername[39m [1mimmediately[0m on the change event rather than waiting for a submit that may never come — opting out should
  take effect when you opt out.

  [1mTesting.[0m No test runner, linter, or formatter exists in this repo today, and [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m has zero dependencies. I'd
  keep that zero-dependency property: [38;5;153mnode:test[39m is built into Node and needs nothing installed. For it to reach
  [38;5;153mpreferences.js[39m, the file gets a small dual-export guard (attach to [38;5;153mwindow[39m when present, [38;5;153mmodule.exports[39m when present) —
  a standard pattern, and the injectable storage backend means tests can drive the fallback and corrupt-data paths
  without a browser. Tests would cover defaults, round-trip, validation rejection, corrupt/tampered entries, unknown
  keys, and the storage-unavailable fallback.

  That's the last open decision — this repo has no tooling at all, and it is cheapest to set up now:

